CD32 interrupt-mask race regression
===================================

Run test-devices-cd-intmask or test-devices-cd-intmask-quick, selected beneath
the native Amiga CD device test group, or run python3 host_test.py directly.
The host compiler needs AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.

The harness compiles the actual CD32_IntEnable/CD32_IntDisable functions
from arch/m68k-amiga/devs/cd/cd32.c. It injects a completion interrupt between
the task's shadow update and hardware store. The interrupt acknowledges its
source through the same production helper. Real interrupt exclusion defers
this event; the hardware model then delivers it once exclusion ends.

All combinations of RXDMA, TXDMA, sector PBX and subcode masks exercise both
enabling and disabling, with and without an outer disabled section. Tests
check the composed task/interrupt result, agreement between the shadow and
hardware, preservation of nesting, and that an acknowledged completion cannot
keep IRQ2 asserted. The model does not duplicate the mask helper code.

For a negative control, pass --source /path/to/original/cd32.c and
--expect-failure. cd32.h must be alongside that source. Assertion failures
must exit 20; sanitizer failures are not accepted as a successful control.

Runtime evidence on a stock CD32 in Copperline 1.1.0: UFO stalls with the
software mask 0x10000000 while Akiko CDINTENA is 0x18000000 and TXDMA is
pending. The handler filters that bit out using the shadow and never masks
it in hardware. Synchronizing the hardware with the shadow releases the
interrupt storm and the intro resumes without changing the allocator.
